<% content_for :page_title, "Data Storage &amp; Privacy" %>

<div class="space-y-8 max-w-4xl">

  <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Data Storage Report</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">Where user data lives: server-side in our PostgreSQL database, and on the user's own device. This is the reference for GDPR / data-protection reporting.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">All app data is stored in <strong>one PostgreSQL database</strong>. Devise (the auth gem) has no separate storage — it manages a subset of columns on the <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">users</code> table; the app owns the rest. No cycle or health data is ever stored on the user's device.</p>
  </div>

  <%# ── Server-side: users table ── %>
  <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6">
    <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-brand-primary flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded bg-brand-field flex items-center justify-center text-sm">1</span>
      Server — <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">users</code> table
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-4">A single row per account. Devise writes only the authentication columns; everything else is written by the app.</p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div>
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mb-2">Devise-managed (authentication)</h4>
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
            <% @devise_columns.each do |col| %>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-2 font-mono text-xs text-gray-800"><%= col.name %></td>
                <td class="py-1.5 text-xs text-gray-500 text-right"><%= col.sql_type %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-2"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">encrypted_password</code> is a bcrypt hash — passwords are never stored in plaintext.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mb-2">App-owned (identity, cycle, preferences)</h4>
        <% @app_column_groups.each do |label, cols| %>
          <h5 class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-primary mt-3 mb-1"><%= label %></h5>
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
              <% cols.each do |col| %>
                <tr>
                  <td class="py-1 pr-2 font-mono text-xs text-gray-800"><%= col.name %></td>
                  <td class="py-1 text-xs text-gray-500 text-right"><%= col.sql_type %></td>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <% unless @uncategorised.empty? %>
      <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 px-4 py-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-amber-800 mb-1">Uncategorised columns (schema changed?)</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-amber-700">
          <% @uncategorised.each do |col| %>
            <span class="font-mono"><%= col.name %></span><%= col == @uncategorised.last ? "." : ", " %>
          <% end %>
          These exist on <code>users</code> but aren't in the categorised lists above.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# ── Server-side: related tables ── %>
  <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6">
    <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-brand-primary flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded bg-brand-field flex items-center justify-center text-sm">2</span>
      Server — health &amp; related data (keyed by <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">user_id</code>)
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-4">These tables hold the user's health and activity data. Every association uses <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">dependent: :destroy</code>, so deleting an account deletes all of these rows with it. Counts are live.</p>
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="text-left text-xs text-gray-500 border-b border-gray-200">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Table</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold text-right">Rows</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
        <% @data_tables.each do |label, count, note| %>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-mono text-xs text-gray-800"><%= label %></td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4 text-right text-xs text-gray-600"><%= count %></td>
            <td class="py-2 text-xs text-gray-500"><%= note %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <%# ── Device-side storage ── %>
  <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6">
    <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-brand-primary flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded bg-brand-field flex items-center justify-center text-sm">3</span>
      User's device
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-4">Nothing sensitive or health-related is stored on the device. Only session/auth cookies, a few non-sensitive UI preferences, and a static-asset cache.</p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div>
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mb-2">Cookies (web + iOS WebView)</h4>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-gray-600">
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">_season_session</code> — encrypted Rails session cookie. Holds the session reference; expires when the browser closes unless "Remember me".</li>
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">remember_user_token</code> — Devise persistent login (default 2 weeks), only if "Remember me" was ticked.</li>
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">csrftoken</code> — CSRF protection token (session-scoped).</li>
          <li>The iOS Turbo Native app keeps the session cookie in the WebView's HTTPCookieStore. No auth token is written to the iOS Keychain (bridge not implemented).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mt-4 mb-2">localStorage / sessionStorage (PWA)</h4>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-xs text-gray-600 list-disc ml-4">
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">holiday_country</code> + <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">holiday_enabled</code> — calendar holiday display preferences.</li>
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">season_last_email</code> — last email used, for PIN-login convenience (cleared on logout).</li>
          <li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">pwaInstallDismissed</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">noCycleDataDismissed</code> — dismissed prompts (session-scoped).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mb-2">Service worker cache (PWA)</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-2"><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">season-v2-v1</code> — static assets only (<code>/</code>, manifest, icons, <code>/assets/*</code>). Offline pages fall back to a static <code>/offline.html</code>. No HTML, JSON, or health responses are cached.</p>

        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mt-4 mb-2">Push notifications</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 mb-2">The device holds the browser's push subscription handle; the endpoint + keys are stored server-side in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">push_subscriptions</code> so we can send notifications.</p>

        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 mt-4 mb-2">WebAuthn / passkeys</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">The private key stays on the device (passkey store). Only the public credential record is stored server-side in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">webauthn_credentials</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ── GDPR notes ── %>
  <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-6">
    <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-brand-primary flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded bg-brand-field flex items-center justify-center text-sm">4</span>
      GDPR notes
    </h3>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-600 list-disc ml-4">
      <li><strong>Deletion</strong> — deleting an account cascades through <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">dependent: :destroy</code> to every table above (see <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">User</code> associations).</li>
      <li><strong>Consent</strong> — health-data processing consent is recorded per user in <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">user_consents</code> and visible on the admin user detail page.</li>
      <li><strong>Device</strong> — no health data, notes, or tokens are written to the device; clearing the browser's site data removes everything client-side.</li>
      <li><strong>Passwords</strong> — stored only as bcrypt hashes (<code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">encrypted_password</code>, <code class="text-xs bg-gray-100 px-1 py-0.5 rounded">pin_digest</code>), never in plaintext.</li>
      <li><strong>Third parties</strong> — Google/Apple/Facebook sign-in stores only the provider's account UID; Google Calendar sync stores access/refresh tokens to sync appointments.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

</div>
